      "topic_id": "6638a2c2-1bbd-4a83-9d68-9b147f87a4d9",
      "text": "What is the primary purpose of formwork for columns and walls?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "To maintain the position and shape of fresh concrete until it sets"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "To serve as the permanent occupancy certificate"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "To replace structural reinforcement"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "To provide the final paint finish only"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "a",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "To maintain the position and shape of fresh concrete until it sets. The audited sources support formwork components, fresh-concrete pressure, scaffolding access, and shoring as temporary support.",
      "explanation": "ρ/g×h is dimensionally incorrect. Curing duration doesn't increase pressure; longer curing allows pressure reduction. Admixture dosage (accelerators/retarders) affects set time, not directly pressure. Concrete form pressure ≈ ρg×h (weight density × gravity × fluid height). For vertical forms, max pressure occurs at depth. Warmer concrete (~30°C) sets faster than cool concrete (~10°C), so strength develops quicker, reducing time the concrete acts as a fluid. Result: lower peak pressure on forms. Placement rate also affects pressure—rapid placement maintains higher fluid height longer.",
      "code_citation": "ACI 347R-14; Concrete Construction Handbook",
